ine_margin": 1.62, "analyst_adjustment": -0.12, "key_factors": ["Recent polls split: WaPo/SSPG shows Taylor up 4, DCCC internal shows Wittman up 1", "Prediction markets price Taylor at 54-55%, modestly favoring Democrats", "Ratings consensus is Tossup, with a few raters still on Lean R", "Wittman's incumbency and $4.3M war chest offset a favorable Democratic environment"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a genuine tossup: a Republican incumbent with a big cash advantage in an R-leaning district, but running into a strong Democratic national environment. The two live polls conflict, markets lean slightly to Taylor, and the ratings consensus sits at Tossup, so a small Democratic edge with wide uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "Any additional independent polling or a shift in the national environment in the final weeks could tip this either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 1st District is one of the cycle's most competitive House races. Republican Rob Wittman, seeking another term after easy wins in past cycles, faces Democrat Shannon Taylor, a prosecutor running an anti-corruption message that has drawn national attention. Wittman holds a large financial edge, with about $4.3 million on hand to Taylor's $1 million, though Democrats have spent modestly against him.\n\nThe polling picture is genuinely mixed: a Washington Post/SSPG survey in late September put Taylor ahead 50-46, while a DCCC internal poll days earlier had Wittman up 47-46. Most expert raters call the race a Tossup, with a few still leaning Republican, and prediction markets price Taylor's chances in the mid-50s. With the district's Republican lean offset by a favorable Democratic environment, the race sits right at the tossup line heading into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "